Building robust ways to outwit MEV strategies by network validators in cryptocurrency applications is garnering rapid interests now. Encrypting mempools with (varieties of) threshold encryption systems is seen as one of the leading solutions to this problem. Particularly, the notion of Batched Threshold Encryption (BTE) proposed in multiple recent papers by Choudhuri et al. [USENIX 2024, 2025] and Agarwal et al. [CRYPTO 2025] gives promising approaches to build scalable encrypted mempools. However, the existing BTE does not address collusion among (potentially malicious) validators (leaking confidential information). We initiate the study of Traceable Batched Threshold Encryption (T-BTE) with the aim to enhance privacy of mempools. T-BTE integrates accountability (via traceability) into BTE, enabling not only thresholdized decryption (via a quorum of some t parties), but additionally with the identification of malicious validators. We present the first definitions and a construction for T- BTE systems satisfying CCA security. In more detail, we build T-BTE from the notion of a Bipartite Batched Threshold Encryption (Bi-BTE) - a primitive we first conceptualize and then build it from bilinear pairings and a one-time signature, and prove its security in the GGM. Our final T-BTE scheme builds upon Bi-BTE and a fingerprinting code (Boneh et al., DRM 2010). We implement and benchmark Bi-BTE and fingerprinting code in Rust to instantiate our T-BTE showcasing its practical potential for encrypted mempools. To the best of our knowledge, our work provides the first implementation of a fingerprinting code.